-- Schema v9 -> v10: remember the MERGE BASE of each account's sync baseline.
--
-- Until now a reconcile that found the remote ahead while this device held unpushed edits could only pick a
-- winner (whole-document last-write-wins) — the loser's edits were dropped. A real merge needs the common
-- ancestor: the snapshot content the account's `last_known_revision` corresponds to. Without it, "the task is
-- absent on this side" is ambiguous between "the other side added it" and "this side deleted it".
--
-- `base_payload` is that ancestor — the serialized PersistedSnapshot of the revision in
-- `last_known_revision`, written by the same statement that advances the revision (push, pull, seed) so the
-- pair can never drift apart. It is per ACCOUNT for exactly the reason the revision is: a base from another
-- account's document would make every entry read as "added/deleted".
--
-- NULL is legitimate and means "no ancestor known" (a DB upgraded from v9, or an account this device has
-- never pushed to / pulled from). SchedulerSyncEngine falls back to the old last-write-wins pull in that
-- case, and the very next successful push/pull fills the column in.

ALTER TABLE account_sync ADD COLUMN base_payload TEXT;
